    "created_at": "2017-02-15T16:23:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-02-15T16:23:41Z",
    "body": "@jeisinger I'm not sure whether to classify this statement as \"wrong\" or not, really.  The particular spec section you link to only ever runs for platform objects, by definition.  In Gecko (and the spec as currently written) it's only relevant for the case when you take a function from one global and invoke it with a \"this\" value from another global.  So the `location.replace.apply(otherLocation, args)` case or whatnot.\r\n\r\nThe general Gecko security model involves, on a conceptual level, performing a security check on any ES internal method invocation, as well as some other places (e.g. there _is_ something like an equivalent of the \"is the this object same-origin with the callee\" check for non-platform objects).  Those checks throw an ES Error, not a \"SecurityError\" DOMException, because they are not actually part of the DOM in any way, but rather part of a general ES security model in SpiderMonkey, and ES has no concept of DOMException.\r\n\r\nI think it's pretty clear based on past discussions that other browsers (esp. Blink) are not interested in implementing Gecko's security model and that Gecko considers what other browsers do to be a security bug in general, which is why we're at an impasse.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
